			"type":"text",
			"text":"# SANER short-paper",
			"styleAttributes":{"border":"invisible"},
			"x":-1240,
			"y":610,
			"width":340,
			"height":80
		},
		{
			"id":"81c4522772d55b41",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"# General evaluation issues:",
			"styleAttributes":{"border":"invisible"},
			"x":-380,
			"y":740,
			"width":900,
			"height":60
		},
		{
			"id":"02bf943c76107a32",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Two analysis can lead to different results (which is not always bad, but can depends on implementation issues)\n- ==network stability==: ping mechanisms make the network unpredictable (eg the client sends updated to the server every tot seconds).  HTTP events can therefore differ between two analysis\n- ==network stability==: failed/successful interaction with a clickable element can trigger novel network events\n- ==DOM loading fails==: loading errors can lead to differences in the collected DOM states\n\nIt is difficult to confirm a vulnerability:\n- ==IDOR== An HTTP response mapped to the same request appears in both runs but in the re-exploration phase we have a different body. It is an IDOR? Or a false positive?\n- ==IDOR== In the re-exploration phase we may have positive HTTP responses (2xx) for requests to third-party domains. Are these IDORs?\n\n==HTTP parameter tampering== does not appear as issue (even if we claim we can detect it):\n- what do we do to demonstrate that we can cover it?\n- shall we replicate an existing CVE about it?",
			"styleAttributes":{"border":"invisible"},
			"x":-380,
			"y":820,
			"width":960,
			"height":560
		}
	],
